---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'current-status', label: 'Current status' },
  { id: 'why-this-is-a-milestone', label: 'Why this is a milestone' },
  { id: 'paired-arithmetic', label: 'Bounding the paired calculation' },
  { id: 'p-values', label: 'Bounding the p-value calculation' },
  { id: 'critical-values', label: 'Selecting critical values for interval work' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What happened next' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'What remains open' },
  { id: 'public-evidence', label: 'Public evidence' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Implementation note"
  title="Bounding numerical error from paired observations to p-values"
  description="We closed reviewed error ledgers for paired arithmetic and Student-t p-values, then selected 200 critical values for candidate confidence-interval work."
  published="September 1, 2026"
  status="Reviewed candidate numerical closure — not a ratified contract or protocol support"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Two major numerical evidence requirements for the proposed paired-t capability
        are now independently review-closed. The first bounds the paired-data arithmetic
        from observations through the test statistic. The second bounds the Student-t
        calculation from that statistic to the p-value.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also selected the exact bytes of 200 reviewed fixed-95% critical values for
        subsequent candidate confidence-interval work. This remains non-authoritative
        engineering work. It is not a ratified numerical contract, a supported
        degrees-of-freedom range, or paired-t protocol support.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why-this-is-a-milestone">
    <h2>Why this is a milestone</h2>
    <p>
      Earlier increments showed that individual tables, boundary cases, execution
      traces, and proof methods worked. This step changes the maturity of the work: two
      complete parts of the proposed numerical contract now have reviewed error rules,
      reviewed execution links, and explicit refusal boundaries.
    </p>
    <p>
      We call these parts ledgers because they answer different questions. Reproducing
      the same binary64 operations shows which calculation ran. Bounding the distance
      from the exact mathematical result shows how much numerical error that calculation
      can have. One cannot substitute for the other.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="paired-arithmetic">
    <h2>Bounding the paired calculation</h2>
    <p>
      The paired-data path starts with explicit pairs and calculates their differences,
      mean difference, sample variance, standard error, test statistic, and integer
      degrees of freedom. Its operation order is fixed, and every binary64 primitive in
      the reviewed trace can be re-executed exactly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mathematical-truth work then places the exact target for each reported quantity
      inside an exact-rational interval and measures the actual trace value against that
      interval. An independent adversarial review reconstructed the arithmetic through a
      separate exact route and completed 568 checks without a failure. This closed the M1
      error requirement for the paired arithmetic candidate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="p-values">
    <h2>Bounding the p-value calculation</h2>
    <p>
      The Student-t path uses positive series forms and a reviewed table of normalization
      constants. Instead of treating the largest error found in a test corpus as a
      universal tolerance, it derives a conservative error bound for the input being
      evaluated. That bound combines binary64 rounding in the executed graph with the
      positive terms left after the series stops.
    </p>
    <p>
      The selected rule also checks the distance to the nearest probability-class
      boundary. If the derived error could change whether a result is normal, subnormal,
      rounded to one, or rounded to zero, an eventual runtime path using this candidate
      rule would have to refuse rather than silently present the class as established.
      Independent review accepted this input-specific rule and closed the M2 Student-t
      tail requirement. No global error constant was selected or required for that
      closure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="critical-values">
    <h2>Selecting critical values for interval work</h2>
    <p>
      A separate reviewed table contains fixed-95% Student-t critical values for every
      integer degree of freedom from 1 through 200. Each mathematical quantile was
      certified inside one exact binary64 rounding cell, and independent review checked
      all 200 cells and their ordering.
    </p>
    <p>
      M3-B selected those exact table bytes for non-authoritative candidate
      confidence-interval work. The ordered-cell content hash is{' '}
      <code>sha256:24ccc86d7a49b9e1ef1e3fc9b038a5b8d338b8b5ca4a02492d8900d7e7dea3c0</code>.
      Selection prevents later candidate work from quietly changing a critical value.
      It does not make 1 through 200 a supported range or turn this into a final Protocol
      table.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="next-step">
    <h2>What happened next</h2>
    <p>
      The subsequent M3-C increment connected the selected critical-value table to the
      reviewed paired-data trace. It records the critical-value lookup and the three
      binary64 operations used to calculate the margin and two confidence-interval
      endpoints. Its independent review returned GO with no findings.
    </p>
    <p>
      That review closes execution identity for the interval calculation, not the
      interval's mathematical-truth bound. M3-D must still propagate the reviewed mean
      and standard-error intervals, critical-value quantization, and endpoint arithmetic
      into one truth envelope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>What remains open</h2>
    <p>
      The confidence-interval endpoint truth requirement and M3 remain open. The work
      also does not select a supported degree-of-freedom maximum, pair or resource
      limits, a supported platform and execution environment, the final supported
      domain, runtime activation, or final public refusal-code spellings.
    </p>
    <p>
      No permanent paired-t identifier, authoritative Public Check, schema, or supported
      bundle has been issued. Release 2 D5 is not complete, Release 2 has not been
      published, and public review issue #25 remains open. The current result is reviewed
      candidate numerical closure, not a user-facing paired-t capability.
    </p>
  </section>

